// case: int-literal-out-of-range
// what: an integer literal that does not fit an orbit_int.
//
// `orbit_int` is a 64-bit `int` (runtime/types.h), so 9223372036854775808 does
// not fit. It used to be accepted, wrapped silently to -9223372036854775808,
// and printed that.
// No diagnostic anywhere: `orbit check` said the program was clean.
//
// It is now an error. The literal did not stop being out of range; it stopped
// being accepted, which is the only thing that was ever wrong with it.
//
// While it was merely recorded, it was recorded as an "int-literal" resolution
// gap, which meant every program containing a large integer printed
// `UNRESOLVED int-literal 1` to stderr. A clean program is supposed to say
// nothing, and a census line is for the gate, not the reader.
//
// The minimum is the one value spelled through the next one up: as the
// negation `-9223372036854775808`, which tests/suite/int_wrap_64.orb avoids and
// tests/std/test_convert.orb prints.

fn main() -> int {
    print(9223372036854775808)
    return 0
}
// expect-error: does not fit an orbit_int
